WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.850
All right, I want to start today with the
coronavirus update, I think it's useful to

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.050
do this at least once a week last week, people
seem to appreciate it.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:13.910
And because I look at this stuff every day,
I assume that everybody has all the same information

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:14.910
I have.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.830
And we learned a long time ago when we were
kids, what happens when one assumes and I

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:19.830
don't want to do that.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.900
So let's do the coronavirus update.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.250
I have cautiously good news about cases and
deaths and vaccines, and we're going to discuss

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.220
the coronavirus variants and let's get right
into it.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:37.960
So first of all cases, last week I told you
that after peaking around January 11th, average

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.559
daily covid cases were down about 42 percent
as of early last week.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:47.900
And I said at the beginning of last week,
by the end of last week, meaning Friday, we

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:54.060
would probably be down 50 percent in terms
of daily cases from that peak on January 11th.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.370
And we reached that milestone and we have
now surpassed that milestone.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.450
Covid average daily cases in the United States
are down to one hundred and 10000, still an

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.680
insane number, one hundred and ten thousand
cases a day on average.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.560
But that's a 56 percent decline from the peak
on January 11th.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.850
So in basically four weeks, average cases
down 56 percent, which puts us roughly where

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.100
we were back on November 10th.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:24.539
And hopefully that will continue to decline
if cases continue declining at the same rate

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:30.319
that they have been since January 11th, we'd
be down to about forty eight thousand cases

00:01:30.319 --> 00:01:32.539
a day by the beginning of March.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:36.859
It's unlikely that this will be a straight
line decline.

00:01:36.859 --> 00:01:39.939
There will be changes, there will be inflection
points.

00:01:39.939 --> 00:01:43.369
But this is a very, very good piece of news.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.560
And we will talk about how the variance may
affect these numbers.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.179
Now, what about deaths?

00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:51.630
We've previously mentioned at this point,
everybody's probably sick of hearing me say

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.969
deaths, lag cases by about three to four weeks.

00:01:55.969 --> 00:02:02.039
We would expect that if cases peak on January
11th, deaths would be peaking and starting

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:03.340
to decline around.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.419
Now, is that happening?

00:02:05.419 --> 00:02:08.289
Yes, it appears to be happening.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:14.000
Deaths hit an average peak of about 3400 deaths
per day around the middle of January, more

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.079
than a 9/11.

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:23.500
Every single day, the trend line sort of plateaued
and it has started to come down and hopefully

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.910
will start to come down more quickly as people
are vaccinated, particularly folks at highest

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.569
risk of death.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:35.420
So that gets us to variance.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.780
And to get to variance, we have to go through
vaccines.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:45.150
Currently, there are two vaccines approved
for emergency use in the United States.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.390
First was the Fizer vaccine, then the Moderna
vaccine.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:58.269
Early concerns about supply and delivery have
been replaced by more cautious optimism from

00:02:58.269 --> 00:03:02.580
Pfizer and Moderna about their ability to
continue delivering millions of doses per

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.580
day.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.890
As many of you know, I was on a sort of on
call list for leftover doses.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.799
At the end of the day, a friend of mine works
at a hospital that has a zero waste commitment

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:14.379
on doses.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:18.569
And I was able to get my first dose of the
Moderna vaccine about 10 days ago from a left

00:03:18.569 --> 00:03:21.160
overdose from canceled appointments.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.079
And the numbers continue to improve.

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:28.319
The U.S. is now giving out an average of one
point four six million doses per day.

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:33.580
Remember, Joe Biden's goal was one million
doses per day for 100 days.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.269
We're at one point for six million doses per
day.

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:42.439
And on Saturday and Sunday, the US actually
did two million doses each day, two million

00:03:42.439 --> 00:03:44.870
on Saturday, two million on Sunday.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.650
That is absolutely fantastic.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.529
If we get a third vaccine approved in the
next three weeks, Johnson and Johnson has

00:03:52.529 --> 00:03:55.180
submitted their vaccine for approval.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.889
That's a one dose vaccine.

00:03:57.889 --> 00:04:04.379
Not inconceivable that the US could get to
2.5 million doses per day.

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:09.810
I mean, I want to be careful, but even three
million doses per day, which would be absolutely

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.810
fantastic.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:17.310
And the one dose nature of the JMJ vaccine
makes the larger the logistics even easier.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.820
Now, cases down, deaths starting to come down,
vaccination starting to go up.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.669
What about the covid variants?

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:29.970
There is a lot of doom and gloom about the
covid variants from the UK, from Brazil and

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.250
from South Africa.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:36.530
Lots of people being very dire about this,
some even saying the vaccines just flat out

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.800
don't work against the variants.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.360
So what is the truth?

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.080
The truth is really not that bad.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:48.050
What we know we know the AstraZeneca vaccine,
which is not even approved in the U.S. yet,

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:51.710
really doesn't seem effective against that
South African variant.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:52.710
That's bad.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.210
They're having problems moving forward.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:58.970
OK, the Pfizer and Moderna vaccines, which
are approved in the United States.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:05.470
Seem the way I would characterize it colloquially
without making a mathematical statement is

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.840
the Pfizer and Moderna vaccines appear to
be quite effective against the variants, including

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:10.860
the South African variant.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:18.849
Now, it is true early data suggests you are
less protected against the South African variant

00:05:18.849 --> 00:05:20.530
with the Pfizer and Moderna vaccines.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.889
It doesn't mean you are unprotected people.

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:28.449
Immune responses on a on a bell curve, as
many of you know and we've looked at.

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:34.690
And those with a stronger immune response
to the vaccines are probably still protected

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.330
against the South African variant and protected
against the British variant.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.640
That's the big one, one seven variant and
the Brazilian variant.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:49.340
But very importantly, this is not a reason
not to get vaccinated.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:52.099
It's a reason to get vaccinated as quickly
as possible.

00:05:52.099 --> 00:05:54.710
The virus mutates as it spreads.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:00.210
Every time that the virus goes from one person
to another is an opportunity for a mutation.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:07.110
The more people you get vaccinated now, fewer
cases, less mutation, fewer variants.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:12.690
OK, Moderna and Pfizer are studying the variants
they are developing as a sort of insurance

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:18.830
policy, a modified booster shot, but that's
eight, nine, 10 months away based on my current

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.099
understanding.

00:06:20.099 --> 00:06:25.770
So we don't yet know what the approval process
will be from an FDA standpoint for modified

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:27.560
vaccines.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.370
So what we can do now is there's no point
in waiting.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:35.120
If you're eligible for a vaccine, get the
vaccine, because if you prevent an infection

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.509
or two infections or three infections by being
vaccinated, even if eventually the British,

00:06:40.509 --> 00:06:46.260
South African and or Brazilian variants become
the dominant strains, the more people that

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.970
have partial protection, the better.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.669
There's no argument for delaying.

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:52.939
There is a debate now about what happens in
three months.

00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:56.139
So we've talked about cases are coming down
quickly.

00:06:56.139 --> 00:07:00.500
Deaths have leveled and are starting to come
down, but the variants are spreading.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.110
There are some the most pessimistic perspective
who say the variants are going to take over.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:10.300
The vaccines will not work against them, and
cases will explode again to the level we saw

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:11.379
in December.

00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:16.570
I don't think that's the most likely scenario
based on all of the epidemiological data I've

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:22.130
seen and interviews and statements and writing
from immunologists and epidemiologists.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:28.789
If we keep vaccinating, the January 11th peak
will likely be the final major peak as the

00:07:28.789 --> 00:07:33.840
weather starts to improve in the parts of
the country that have winter and more and

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:34.889
more people are vaccinated.

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:40.930
Yes, it is likely true that more and more
of the cases will be the variants, but it

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:45.300
is also true that more and more millions and
tens of millions and hopefully hundreds of

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.830
millions of Americans will have sufficient
protection against these variants.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:52.210
That cases will continue to decline.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:08:00.020
And now when we get to then we'll have summer,
et cetera, when we get to the next flu season,

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:06.270
in the fall, hopefully by then there will
be a modified booster, if indeed it's determined

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.150
that that's what's necessary.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:14.219
And my hope is, and I believe it's realistic
but cautious, is that the January 11th peak

00:08:14.219 --> 00:08:18.530
was the final peak for this pandemic.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:23.780
All we can do now is continue to be safe,
continue to vaccinate.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:27.909
And I've mentioned this before, we should
not undersell the vaccine.

00:08:27.909 --> 00:08:31.681
I know people who are saying, listen, I'm
being told even if you get vaccinated, it

00:08:31.681 --> 00:08:34.680
may not work against the variants and you
still have to wear a mask.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.480
You still have to social distance.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.620
There are lots of good immunologists and epidemiologists
who are saying, listen, in a month, if the

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:46.580
numbers continue going this way, if you are
fully vaccinated, meaning a week past dose,

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.690
too, it's it's a very acceptable level of
risk to have an indoor dinner with six fully

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.060
vaccinated people.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.120
Let's be realistic about what this means.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.460
It doesn't mean you go to the grocery store
and you say, I don't have to wear a mask because

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:00.460
I'm vaccinated.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.200
That's going to be the policy for some time
in parts of the country.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.700
I know friends in places like Arizona are
telling me nobody's wearing a mask at the

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.670
grocery store, but in much of the country,
that's what's going on.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:11.820
It'll continue to go on.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:16.200
But let's be realistic about the game changing
nature of these vaccines.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:10:37.560
Will check in about the data again next week.

